# How to center text in PHPExcel merged cells

When merging cells in PHPExcel, the default behavior often leaves you with a visual mismatch that breaks the layout of your spreadsheet. Specifically, when you merge a range like `A1:B1` and set the value of the first cell (the top-left anchor), the content aligns to the left by default. If your goal is to center that text horizontally within the merged span, the solution isn't found in the merging logic itself, but in the worksheet style configuration.

The provided code snippet demonstrates a common setup: creating a new workbook, setting a value at column 0, row 1, and then merging cells A1 through B1. While this successfully creates the merged region, it inherits the default alignment properties of the cell. To achieve the desired centered look, you must explicitly define the alignment style for the specific range or the entire sheet.

The core mechanism relies on PHPExcel's `getStyle()` method. You need to access the style object for the target range and set the horizontal alignment property to `'center'`. This is a distinct step from merging; the merge command defines the physical boundary of the cell, while the style command defines how content inside that boundary is rendered.

Here is how you can modify the code to achieve this:

```php
$objPHPExcel = new PHPExcel();
$sheet = $objPHPExcel->getActiveSheet();

// Set the value and merge the cells first
$sheet->setCellValueByColumnAndRow(0, 1, "test");
$sheet->mergeCells('A1:B1');

// Apply horizontal alignment to the merged range
$styleArray = array(
    'alignment' => array(
        'horizontal' => PHPExcel_Style_Alignment::HORIZONTAL_CENTER,
        // Optional: You might also want to center vertically
        'vertical'   => PHPExcel_Style_Alignment::VERTICAL_CENTER,
        'wraptext'   => false // Prevents wrapping if the text is short
    )
);

// Apply the style directly to the merged cells
$sheet->getStyle('A1:B1')->getAlignment()->applyFromArray($styleArray);

$objWriter = PHPExcel_IOFactory::createWriter($objPHPExcel, 'Excel2007');
$objWriter->save("test.xlsx");
```

It is important to note that applying styles after merging is the most reliable approach. If you attempt to set alignment on a single cell before merging, the resulting style might not propagate correctly across the entire merged area depending on how PHPExcel handles the internal representation of merged cells. By selecting the full range `A1:B1` and applying the alignment style, you ensure that the rendering engine treats the whole block as a single unit with consistent formatting.

You can also apply this to an entire sheet if the default behavior is undesirable across the board. In such cases, calling `$sheet->getStyle()->getAlignment()->setHorizontal(PHPExcel_Style_Alignment::HORIZONTAL_CENTER)` will affect every cell in that worksheet. However, be cautious when mixing merged and non-merged cells; explicit styling per range prevents unexpected inconsistencies where some cells appear centered and others left-aligned.

One potential pitfall involves the interaction with vertical alignment. If your text is long or if you are dealing with headers that need to fill a tall column, simply centering horizontally might leave the text floating in the middle of a large cell. In these scenarios, adding `VERTICAL_CENTER` ensures the content sits squarely in the middle of both dimensions. Conversely, for compact data grids, leaving vertical alignment as default (top) often reads better for tabular data.

While PHPExcel is a powerful tool for generating spreadsheets, it has reached its end-of-life status. If you are building new applications requiring complex Excel generation, libraries like PhpSpreadsheet offer a more active maintenance cycle and improved API ergonomics. However, the logic for handling merged cell alignment remains consistent across both: merge first, then apply the style explicitly to the resulting range.
